  "client_view_text": "  A write-ahead log is a file that keeps track of all changes to the database.  Here's a paragraph describing the normal operation of a database with a write-ahead log:\n\nThe database engine makes changes to the database in two phases.  First, it writes the change to the write-ahead log.  Then, it applies the change to the database itself.  For example, if the user inserts a new record, the database engine first writes a log entry indicating the insertion, and then it physically inserts the record into the database.\n\nHere's a paragraph describing how a database recovers after a crash:\n\nWhen the database engine restarts after a crash, it reads through the write-ahead log, applying each change to the database in the order they were originally recorded.  For example, if the user had inserted a new record just before the crash, the database engine will read the corresponding log entry and reapply the insertion to the database, effectively restoring the database to the state it was in just before the crash.\n\n## Step 1: Database operation with write-ahead log\nThe database engine operates in two phases when making changes to the database. First, it writes the change to the write-ahead log. This ensures that the change is recorded before it is applied to the database"
